<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Veit Stoss</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1447–<a href="/years/1533">1533</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Wit Stwosz</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Veit Stoss was a German sculptor and woodcarver of the late Gothic period, active mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>. His masterpiece, the high altar of St. Mary's Basilica in Kraków, was completed in 1489. Back in Nuremberg he was branded on both cheeks for forgery in 1503, but went on to carve works such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annunciation">Annunciation</a> hanging in the church of St. Lorenz.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/veit-stoss.jpg" alt="Veit Stoss" width="195" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Hoss at German Wikipedia. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Veit_Sto%C3%9F.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Veit Stoss, also spelled Stoß and known in Polish as Wit Stwosz, was a German sculptor who worked mostly in wood, and his career spanned the transition between the late Gothic and the Northern Renaissance. His style emphasised pathos and emotion, helped by his virtuoso carving of billowing drapery, and has been called late Gothic Baroque. Documents give his birthplace as Horb or Horbn, which most researchers identify with Horb am <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neckar">Neckar</a>, though traces in his art suggest an early education in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, and his brother was certainly born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aarau">Aarau</a>. Nothing is known for certain of his life before 1473, when he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> and married Barbara Hertz.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1477 Stoss moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>, where he lived and worked until 1496. The altar of St. Mary's Church, completed in 1489, was the largest triptych of its time, and like his other large works it required a workshop that included painters and gilders. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> he also made the tomb of Casimir IV in Wawel Cathedral and the marble tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zbigniew-olesnicki">Zbigniew Oleśnicki</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gniezno">Gniezno</a>, and some of this work used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> classical ornament. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, on the order of Hans Frank, the dismantled altar was shipped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> around 1941. It was found in 1945 in the basement of Nuremberg Castle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and, after restoration in Poland, was put back in the basilica ten years later.</p><p>Stoss returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> in 1496 with his wife and eight children. In 1503 he was arrested for forging the seal and signature of a fraudulent contractor, was branded on both cheeks and was forbidden to leave the city. Emperor Maximilian pardoned him in 1506, though the council shunned him and he received few large commissions afterwards. He made an altar for Bamberg Cathedral, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annunciation">Annunciation</a> and Tobias and the Angel. In 1516 he made a wooden statue of Saint Roch for the Basilica of Santissima Annunziata in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giorgio-vasari">Giorgio Vasari</a> praised as a miracle in wood.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Stoss was buried in the St. Johannis cemetery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a>. His son Stanisław worked as a woodcarver in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> from 1505, and three other sons settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a>: Johann as a painter and woodcarver in Schäßburg, the goldsmith Martin in Medwisch, and Veit the Younger in Kronstadt. A Polish novel of 1913 and a film of 1961, The Story of a Yellow Crakow, were based on his life.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Veit Stoss” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Albrecht Dürer, <b>Veit Stoss</b>, Hans Sachs and Martin Behaim, maker of the oldest surviving globe, worked there.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-nuremberg">Free Imperial City of Nuremberg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Horb am Neckar, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.45,8.69&amp;year=1502" aria-label="Horb am Neckar on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4752 470 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Veit Stoss"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5202 740h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5202 740h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.45,8.69&amp;year=1502">What was here in 1502? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
